  "hero.kicker": "Computing & AI · Quantum Computing · From First Principles to the Frontier",
  "hero.title": "What Quantum Computers Can Actually Do",
  "hero.sub": "What they can do that ordinary computers cannot<br>is less than the hype promises, and more concrete than skeptics assume",
  "hero.lead": "News stories often claim that a quantum computer “tries every answer at once” and therefore computes everything fast. Where does that explanation break down? This essay asks a single question: <strong>what can a quantum computer actually do that a classical computer cannot?</strong> We start with one qubit and follow the thread up to experimental records in 2026. Embedded in this page is a real state-vector simulator (up to 10 qubits) implementing complex amplitudes, standard quantum gates, measurement, and noise straight from the textbook — every chart in these chapters is computed live by it. By the end, you should be able to read a quantum-computing headline and know which numbers to check first.",
  "hero.note": "This page was written by an AI. The simulator is real, though small: results up to 10 qubits are computed exactly, while larger scales are extrapolated from formulas (the text notes which numbers come from the simulator and which come from the literature). Frontier entries in Chapter 11 are dated and sourced in the footer; vendor roadmaps and announcements are marked as “Vendor claims”, not established facts. Nothing here predicts timelines or offers investment advice.",

  /* ───── 01 ───── */
  "ch1.title": "One Qubit Is Two Complex Numbers",
  "ch1.law": "The state of one qubit is a pair of complex amplitudes. A measurement returns only one classical bit, with each outcome appearing with probability equal to the squared magnitude of its amplitude.",
  "ch1.p1": "Think first of a tossed coin still spinning in the air. When you call it “half heads, half tails”, that only reflects your ignorance: it already has a definite face up, and you simply have not looked. A qubit is not like that.",
  "ch1.p2": "A qubit's state is written α|0⟩ + β|1⟩. Here α and β are complex numbers (points on the plane — see <a href=\"../../../math/algebra/complex-numbers/\">Complex Numbers · Rotation on the Plane</a>) called <strong>amplitudes</strong>, satisfying |α|² + |β|² = 1. Pairs (α, β) that differ only by an overall phase represent the same physical state, leaving just two real angles: θ sets how much of |0⟩ versus |1⟩ is present, and φ sets the relative phase between them. So the state can be written cos(θ/2)|0⟩ + e<sup>iφ</sup>sin(θ/2)|1⟩. Treating θ as latitude and φ as longitude places every pure state on a sphere called the <strong>Bloch sphere</strong>.",
  "ch1.model.title": "Prepare one qubit and measure it repeatedly",
  "ch1.model.how": "Drag θ and φ to point the state vector anywhere on the sphere; choose a measurement axis, then click “Measure 1×” or “Measure 100×”. Each shot prepares a fresh identical qubit before measuring, because measurement alters the state. Watch the observed frequencies converge toward the probabilities predicted by the amplitudes.",
  "ch1.model.aria": "Qubit state on the Bloch sphere and measurement outcome counts",
