  "ch3.cv.win": "Win rate",
  "ch3.cv.measured": "Observed",
  "ch3.cv.theory": "Theory",
  "ch3.say.none": "No rounds played yet. With these angles, the simulator's exact theoretical win rate is {w}%.",
  "ch3.say.few": "Played {n} rounds with win rate {f}%. Sample size is still small (random fluctuation is roughly ±{se} percentage points); play more rounds to settle it. Theoretical win rate: {w}%.",
  "ch3.say.beat": "Won {k} of {n} rounds (<em>{f}%</em>), beating the 75% classical limit. Theoretical win rate for these angles: {w}%.",
  "ch3.say.below": "Win rate over {n} rounds is {f}%, below 75%: these angles only reach {w}% in theory. Try clicking “Optimal angles”.",
  "ch3.say.close": "Win rate over {n} rounds is {f}%. The theoretical rate {w}% is only slightly above 75%, so more rounds are needed to separate them.",
  "ch3.say.classical": "Classical “both answer 0” strategy: {f}% over {n} rounds. It loses whenever x = y = 1, locking its long-run win rate at 75% no matter how long you play.",
  "ch3.say.classicalNone": "Classical “both answer 0” strategy: loses only on x = y = 1, for a long-run win rate of 75%. Click “Play 1000 rounds” to test.",
  "ch3.p3": "The optimal angles are 0° and 45° for Alice, 22.5° and −22.5° for Bob, giving a win rate of cos²22.5° ≈ 85.4%. Written in terms of correlations across the four input pairs, the CHSH sum is S = 2√2 ≈ 2.83, exceeding the classical bound of 2. This is a <strong>violation of Bell's inequality</strong>: the outcomes exhibit correlations impossible to reproduce with any pre-written lookup table. The 2022 Nobel Prize in Physics recognized the experiments that confirmed this.",
  "ch3.p4": "Can Alice use this to send a message to Bob? Check the final readout: regardless of which angle Alice chooses, Bob's own stream of 1s averages 50%. The simulator's exact marginal is strictly 50%; any discrepancy you see is sampling noise. The correlation only becomes visible when Alice and Bob later compare notes — which requires ordinary communication. Therefore, entanglement <strong>cannot transmit information faster than light</strong>.",
  "ch3.p5": "Why does entanglement matter for computation? A Bell state cannot be factored into “(state of qubit 1) × (state of qubit 2)”. Once many qubits are entangled, describing the full system requires assigning an amplitude to every joint bitstring combination. The next chapter counts just how many.",
  "ch3.limit": "Entanglement and detectors here are ideal. Real Bell tests must close loopholes — such as missed photons or insufficient separation between the two stations.",

  /* ───── 04 ───── */
  "ch4.title": "n Qubits Take 2ⁿ Complex Numbers",
  "ch4.law": "Describing n qubits takes 2ⁿ complex amplitudes, so simulating them classically scales exponentially with n — yet measuring them still returns only n bits, so having many amplitudes does not automatically mean fast computation.",
  "ch4.p1": "Three classical bits sit in one of 8 combinations at any moment, taking 3 bits to write down. A general three-qubit state assigns a complex amplitude to all 8 combinations at once. Every extra qubit doubles the list of amplitudes.",
  "ch4.model.title": "How much memory does a quantum state take?",
  "ch4.model.how": "Top: drag n (1 to 60) to see the memory needed to store an n-qubit state vector (16 bytes per complex number). Bottom: an actual n-qubit state in this page's simulator (n ≤ 10, produced by a fixed random circuit), showing each bitstring's probability. Click “Measure once” to see how much you actually extract.",
  "ch4.model.aria1": "Memory required to store an n-qubit state vector on a log scale",
  "ch4.model.aria2": "Probabilities of all 2^n bitstrings for an n-qubit state in the simulator",
